# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  JACK: Marswalk — optional environment configuration
#
#  EVERYTHING IN HERE IS OPTIONAL. With no .env file at all the game is
#  complete: MERV, the rover companion, answers from this project's own sourced
#  Mars knowledge base and the live simulation telemetry, and speaks through
#  the browser's built-in speech engine. Nothing leaves the machine.
#
#  Copy to `.env.local` and fill in only what you want.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- MERV's voice ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# How the rover's speech is wired. Three tiers:
#
#   local     No credentials. Sourced answers from this repo, spoken by the
#             browser. This is the default and it always works.
#   grounded  Deepgram speech-to-text in and text-to-speech out. The ANSWERS
#             still come from this project's knowledge base and telemetry, so
#             every fact keeps its citation. Recommended.
#   agent     Deepgram's Voice Agent: their speech recognition, a hosted
#             language model, and their speech synthesis over one socket.
#             Open-ended conversation, grounded by injecting live telemetry
#             into the prompt and by giving the model functions that query
#             this simulation. Costs money per spoken word, so it is never
#             selected automatically — ask for it by name.
#
# 'auto' (the default) picks 'grounded' when a credential is available and
# 'local' when it is not. It never picks 'agent' on its own.
VITE_ROVER_MODE=auto

# --- Deepgram credentials --------------------------------------------------
#
# READ THIS BEFORE SETTING A KEY.
#
# A browser cannot keep a secret. Any variable beginning with VITE_ is compiled
# into the public JavaScript bundle, so putting a real API key in
# VITE_DEEPGRAM_API_KEY and deploying it is the same as publishing the key.
# The app refuses to use that variable unless the page is on localhost.
#
# For anything deployed, use the token endpoint instead. Two implementations
# ship with this repo — api/deepgram-token.ts for Vercel and
# netlify/functions/deepgram-token.mts for Netlify. Both read a SERVER-side
# DEEPGRAM_API_KEY (note: no VITE_ prefix, so it never reaches the browser) and
# hand out tokens that expire in sixty seconds.

# 1. PREFERRED — your own endpoint that mints short-lived tokens.
#    With either bundled function, this is the path it serves on:
# VITE_DEEPGRAM_TOKEN_URL=/api/deepgram-token

# 2. The server-side secret the function above reads. NOT prefixed with VITE_,
#    so it stays out of the bundle. Set this in your host's environment
#    variable settings rather than in a committed file.
# DEEPGRAM_API_KEY=

# 3. LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY — the raw key, in the bundle. Ignored unless the
#    page is served from localhost.
# VITE_DEEPGRAM_API_KEY=

# --- Voices and models (all optional) --------------------------------------
#
# Deepgram Aura voice for MERV. Orion is a low, even male voice that carries
# over HUD noise; aura-2-thalia-en and aura-2-arcas-en are good alternatives.
# VITE_ROVER_VOICE=aura-2-orion-en

# Speech-to-text model.
# VITE_ROVER_LISTEN_MODEL=nova-3

# Language model for the 'agent' tier. Deepgram hosts these, so a Deepgram key
# is the only credential needed — there is no second provider account and no
# second secret to leak. Haiku is the default because latency is what makes a
# companion feel present, and a slow companion is a worse one.
# VITE_ROVER_THINK_PROVIDER=anthropic
# VITE_ROVER_THINK_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5

# --- Development -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Publishes window.__jack and window.__live for browser automation. Dev only.
# VITE_ENABLE_DEBUG_GLOBALS=true

# --- Multiplayer crew link -------------------------------------------------
#
# Render-hosted builds use their own /ws endpoint automatically. GitHub Pages
# has no backend, so add this as a GitHub Actions repository variable before
# the Pages build runs (Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions → Variables).
# Use wss:// in production; ws:// is accepted only for local development.
# VITE_MULTIPLAYER_URL=wss://your-render-service.onrender.com/ws

# Render validates WebSocket Origin headers. The public GitHub Pages origin is
# allowed by default. Add comma-separated origins here if you use custom domains.
# This is a SERVER variable, not a VITE variable.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://rohit-ats.github.io,https://your-domain.example
